<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0367jzzfpr> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Europe" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ dcterms:isPartOf <http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/79745128> ;
            bibo:locator "page 65" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q46> ;
    skos:altLabel "Second Transition"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "The label assigned to the period and the reference to historians of modern Europe clearly indicates that this period refers to European history." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0367jz> ;
    skos:note "\"To save the meaning of \"mediaeval\" and at the same time to give greater clearness and validity to both \"ancient\" and \"modern\", it is worth while to carry on our process of division a stage further and to create for our use two additional periods, one preceding, one following that of the Middle Ages proper. If we think of this purely mediaeval section as beginning with Karl the Great about the year 800 and ending with Dante about 1300 these two new periods will then define themselves chronologically as covering respectively the years from about 400 to 800 and from about 1300 to 1600\" (pp. 63-64); \"At first thought these two added periods seem to be lacking in our first essential requirement, a distinctive character; but this difficulty grows less upon reflection. Superficially they seem to be characterized only by a greater degree of confusion than the others but, as we try to penetrate this apparent confusion, we see that it is better described as \"transition\"...\" (p. 64); \"And what has been said of the First European Transition is equally true of the second\" (p. 65); \"Small wonder that this period was until lately neglected by the general student of history or that it has been claimed alternately by the historians of the Middle Ages and by those of modern Europe. It becomes interesting and illuminating when it is treated by itself as the Second European Transition... These two added periods, then, have a character of their own, the character of transition\" (p. 65)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Second European Transition" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "about 1600" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1600"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "about 1300" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1300"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

