<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsf9hgx> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Egypt and Ethiopia" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ bibo:locator "Row labeled \"Africa\"" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1049>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q115>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q79> ;
    skos:altLabel "Ethiopians"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Although neither the text nor the timeline are clear about chronology between 946 (when \"the Egyptians, with the assistance of the Jews and Phoenicians, made Osarsiphus king, expelling the Ethiopians from Lower Egypt\" (probably the small block at the beginning of the first \"Ethiopian\" section for Egypt in the timeline) and 671, the timeline suggests more precise divisions than the text. The timeline has two different \"Ethiopian\" divisions, both of which seem to distinguish between Egypt proper and \"Ethiopia\"; for the middle \"Ethiopian\" division, which begins between 780 BC (for \"Ethiopia\") and 750 BC (for Egypt), it is very likely that Priestley is referring to the 25th (Kushite/Nubian) Dynasty and the Kushite conquest of Upper Egypt." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsf> ;
    skos:note "From \"A description of a new chart of History\", 6th ed., 1786, s.v. \"Ethiopia and Abyssinia\", p. 87: \"This country was ravaged by Sesac, or Sesostris the Egyptian, in the reign of his father Ammon, about 1008 B.C., but upon his death, in 956, the Ethiopians recovered their independency, and in 947 they invaded Egypt... In 671 Ethiopia was conquered by Asserhaddon king of Assyria, but it revolted upon his death in 668.\"" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ethiopians" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "671 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0670"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "956 to 947 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "-0955"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "-0946"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsf9j6t> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Corea" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ dcterms:isPartOf <http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/82293509> ;
            bibo:locator "Row labeled \"China\"" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q483134> ;
    skos:altLabel "Corea"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote """The chart depicts years with a chronological line at the top, radiating from a center point of the birth of Christ. "BC" has been added to the entries where required as illustrated by the structure of the chart, but not actually written.
Spatial coverage has been determined by the labels on the chart running along the right side, separated across the chart with horizontal lines, and narrowed within each horizontal line by smaller areas.

The text is entirely uncertain about when Korea became independent from early Japanese domination, but the timeline places at dotted (uncertain) line at around 650. Spatial coverage is the entire Korean peninsula.""" ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsf> ;
    skos:note "From \"A description of a new chart of History\", 6th ed., 1786, s.v. \"Korea\", p. 86: \"In 112 B.C. Ki-tze is said to have been the first king of this country, but in 201 after Christ, it was conquered by the Japanese, and it is uncertain when it recovered its independency. In 1224 it became tributary to Jenghis Khan, and in 1664 was conquered by Shunchi, the first prince of the second Tartar dynasty.\"" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Corea" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1664" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1664"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "around 650" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0650"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

