    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q159>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q711> ;
    skos:altLabel "Eluth Mongols"@en,
        "Kalmyks"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "\"Eluths\" appear in 18th and 19th century sources as a western Mongol ethnic sub-division and/or a synonym for Kalmyks, although they appear to be active further east than the area occupied by Kalmyks in the later Russian empire. Since the Eluths are still shown as independent in the timeline, and since the Kalmyks were absorbed by China on one side and the Russian empire on the other in 1771, the Kalmyks are probably the people to whom Priestley is referring." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsf> ;
    skos:note "From \"A description of a new chart of History\", 6th ed., 1786, p. 84: \"The Eluths became a separate state about 1400, and continue independent to this day.\"" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eluths" ;
