<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsfwxqw> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Southern China" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ dcterms:isPartOf <http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/82293509> ;
            bibo:locator "Row labeled \"China\"" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    skos:altLabel "Chinese"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote """The chart depicts years with a chronological line at the top, radiating from a center point of the birth of Christ. "BC" has been added to the entries where required as illustrated by the structure of the chart, but not actually written.
Spatial coverage has been determined by the labels on the chart running along the right side, separated across the chart with horizontal lines, and narrowed within each horizontal line by smaller areas.

Strangely, Priestley pays almost no attention to the well-documented history of southern China; although he mentions the Song Dynasty, he only provides an end-date -- the beginning of Chinese history in the timeline is just the edge of the horizontal bar and the legend "Chinese". We have used the start of that horizontal bar as an arbitrary start-date.
""" ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsf> ;
    skos:note "From \"A description of a new chart of History\", 6th ed., 1786, s.v. \"China\", p. 85: \"This empire is certainly of very great antiquity; but, like other antient nations, has been aggrandized by fables. The first monarch is said to have been Fohi, whom the defenders of Chinese antiquities pretend to have been the fame with Noah; but omitting these pretended antiquities, the first foreign invasion of China was made by the Kitans in 946... [Oktay] attacked the Song in 1235, and made them tributary in 1260, but they were finally conquered by Kublay in 1279.\"" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chinese" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1260" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "1260"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "1279"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "earlier than 80 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0080"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsfx8t4> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "the whole region between China and Indostan; Thibet" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ dcterms:isPartOf <http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/82293509> ;
            bibo:locator "Row labeled \"China\"" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q17252>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q711>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q837> ;
    skos:altLabel "Great Empire of Tangut"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote """The chart depicts years with a chronological line at the top, radiating from a center point of the birth of Christ. "BC" has been added to the entries where required as illustrated by the structure of the chart, but not actually written.
Spatial coverage has been determined by the labels on the chart running along the right side, separated across the chart with horizontal lines, and narrowed within each horizontal line by smaller areas. In the case of the spatial coverage of the value "Thibet", which for Priestley does not seem to match modern national boundaries, we have used the countries where the geopolitical phenomena he associates with "Thibet" (e.g. the Tangut Empire) took place.
Ending dates of 1770 derived from the year of publication, and also the point at which the chart ends.""" ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p05hrsf> ;
    skos:note "From \"A description of a new chart of History\", 6th ed., 1786, p. 84: \"In 630 Ki-tzon, king of Tufan or Sifan, possessed a vast empire in this part of Asia, including the whole region between China and Indostan; but it began to decay about 842, the emperor Itay having no issue. In 1015 it was divided into several states, which were all conquered by Jenghis Khan in 1227. At what time this country became independent of the Tartars is not known, but in 1716 Thibet, the remains of it, was conquered by the Chinese.\"" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Great Empire of Tangut" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1227" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1227"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "630" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0630"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

