<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h586b> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Sogdiana or Sogdia, Uzbekistan" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q265>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q813>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q863> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x115843> ;
    skos:altLabel "Sogdian"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "Central Asian people of Sogdiana or Sogdia, centred on the fertile Zeravsham valley around Samarqand in Uzbekistan. It was a province of the Achaemenids (q.v.), invaded by Alexander the Great (q.v.), and was part of the Bactrian Greek kingdom until the Sogdians appear to have broken away c.200 BC and begun minting their own coins. The region was subsequently invaded by the Indo-Scythian (q.v.) and Yuezhi (q.v.) nomadic tribes in the 2nd century BC, but nevertheless remained a prosperous centre until the Mongol (q.v.) invasions in the early 13th century.  The Sogdians occupied a key position along the ancient Silk Road and, from the early 3rd century BC until the 10th century AD, played a major role in facilitating trade between China and Central Asia. They were the dominant merchants, their language became the lingua franca of the Silk Road, and they played an important role not only in trade but also in the spread of philosophies and religions, such as Manichaeism and Buddhism, to the east." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sogdian" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "early 13th century" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "1201"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "1234"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c. 200 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0199"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x115843> .

