<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hgrmh> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Cyprus" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q229> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x14149> ;
    skos:altLabel "Late Cypriot"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "1650 BC-1050 BC.  The third major division of the Cypriot Bronze Age is marked by the appearance of new pottery types, such as White Slip, Base Ring and Monochrome in the North West of the island, which gradually replace older fabrics which continue in use from the Middle Bronze Age. Other important fabrics included Bichrome Wheelmade, Red Lustrous Wheelmade and various Plain and Painted Wheelmade wares; the latter are heavily influenced by imports from the Mycenaean world in particular (but also the Levant) which gradually displace the older hand-made wares by the 12th century.  The period is characterised by a thriving copper extraction industry, intense commercial interchange with surrounding regions – with imports visible from all around the Eastern Mediterranean - , and the emergence of more complex forms of social organisation, which can be called towns by LCIIC and LCIIIA. Literacy is attested in the form of documents written in a script called Cypro-Minoan, which has some links with Bronze Age Crete.  The last phase of the LC period, LCIII is a transitional period marked by major changes in the settlement pattern, burial practices, economy and material culture (including pottery types); the development of Proto-White Painted and Proto-Bichrome wares signal the beginning of the Iron Age in ceramic terms. The development of iron-making technology, in which Cypriot metallurgists played a leading role, develops apace at this time." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Late Cypriot" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1050 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-1049"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1650 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-1649"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x14149> .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hgs5w> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Persia, Iraq and Syria" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q794>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q796>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q858> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x41057> ;
    skos:altLabel "Seljuq dynasty"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "1040-1194 (AH 431-AH 590). Turkic dynasty ruling in Persia, Iraq and Syria that  reunited central 'Abbasid lands. The Seljuqs had originally their home in the steppes north of the Caspian and Aral Seas. They converted to Islam at the end of the 10th century. Under Toghril I Beg they seized Nishapur, the capital of the Ghaznavids in 1038 (AH 429). He entered Baghdad in 1055 (AH 447) and had his title of sultan confirmed by the caliph. The sultanate of the Great Seljuqs was based on a hierarchically organised state in the Perso-Islamic monarchic system, with the supreme sultan supported by a Perian and Arab beaurocracy and a multi-national army directed by Turkish slave commanders. Under Alp Arslan and his son Malik Shah the Great Seljuq empire reached its zenith and Isfahan was the capital. The Seljuqs founded various orthodox Sunni madrasas or religious colleges in Irag and the Persian lands and encouraged a synthesis of traditional theological and legal studies with the spirit of Sufism." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seljuq dynasty" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1194 (AH 590)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1194"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1040 (AH 431)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1040"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x41057> .

