<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hhcgp> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Egypt and Damascus, Syria" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q79>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q858> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x23175> ;
    skos:altLabel "Mamluk dynasty"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "1250-1517 (AH 648-AH 923). The Mamluks (meaning 'one possessed, slave') arose from the Turkic troops of Ayyubids of Egypt and Damascus. Two lines of Mamluk sultans are distinguished: the Bahri Mamluks and the Burji Mamluks. The Bahris were ethnically Turkic from the southern Russian steppes with a mixture of other races including Kurds and also Mongols. The Burjis were Circassians from the Christian areas of the northern Caucasus. The Mamluks continued the strongly Sunni policy of the Ayyubids with the sultans, governors and amirs founding numerous mosques, madrasas and other religious and charitable institutions in Cairo, Damascus, Aleppo and elsewhere. They saw their role as defenders of Islam against the infidel Mongols, Frankish crusaders and heterodox Muslims in the mountains of western Syria. Egypt and Syria enjoyed considerable prosperity under the Mamluks until the 15th century." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mamluk dynasty" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1517 (AH 923)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1517"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1250 (AH 648)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1250"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x23175> .

