<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hmdw6> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Greece, Cyprus, eastern Balkans, parts of the Caucasus, Asia Minor, Levant, Egypt and parts of Italy" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x41013> ;
    skos:altLabel "Late Antique"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "The term is traditionally used for the former territories of the Eastern Roman Empire, ie. Greece, Cyprus, eastern Balkans, parts of the Caucasus (Georgia, Armenia, etc.), Asia Minor, Levant, Egypt and parts of Italy, for the 6thC and 7thC AD. For the 4th and 5thC AD, the term Late Roman is preferred. The term ‘Byzantine’ can be used as an alternative .  The term ‘Late Antique’ is not used for the material culture of the former territories of the western Roman empire, ie. Italy, Gaul, Hispania, Britannia and Western North Africa (Mauritania, Tripolitania), for the 6th and 7thC AD. Instead, the term Early Medieval is preferred. Neither is 'Lare Antique' used for the broadly contemporary Sasanian Empire to the East (for which 'Sasanian' is preferred) or 'Early Islamic' and its Narrow Terms.  For non-Roman material (post AD 410) from Britain, the terms 'Sub-Roman' or 'Early Medieval' are used in preference to 'Late Antique'.  N.B. The Department of Greek and Roman Antiquities does not use the term 'Late Antique', see instead the terms 'Roman' and 'Late Roman'." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Late Antique" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "7th century AD" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "0601"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "0700"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "6th century AD" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "0501"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "0600"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x41013> .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hmf57> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "most of Anatolia" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q43> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x52027> ;
    skos:altLabel "Seljuqs of Rum"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "1081-1307 (AH 473-AH 707). Turkic dynasty which originated in west-central Anatolia with capital at Konya but later spread over most of Anatolia except the western fringes. The Seljuqs of Rum were an offshoot of the Great Seljuqs. After Alp Arslan's victory over the Byzantines, one of the descendants of one of the sons established a local Seljuq sultanate in Anatolia. The Latin conquest of Constantinople in 1204 (AH 600) gave the Seljuqs and oppurtunity to reestablish their power and extend their influence to the Mediterranean. With this and control over northern coastlands a flourishing transit trade developed between Egypt and the eastern Mediterranean across Anatolia to the Black Sea, the Crimea and the lands of the Mongol Golden Hoard. After circa 1225 commercial relations began with Italian trading cities." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seljuqs of Rum" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1307 (AH 707)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1307"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1081 (AH 473)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1081"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x52027> .

