<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hn6p3> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Europe and North America" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q46>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q49> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x117615> ;
    skos:altLabel "Last Ice Age"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "The Ice Age refers to the period between 2.6 million and 11,700 years ago during which climate varied through alternating warm and cold periods. Modern humans and many other mammal species evolved during this period.  During some cold stages (glaciations) temperatures dropped so much that continental and alpine ice sheets spread over vast areas. With water frozen to ice, sea levels also dropped providing humans and animals with opportunities to spread out across land bridges which were then submerged again during warm periods such as we live in today. Throughout the Ice Age, Britain formed part of mainland of Europe.  However, 'Ice Age' (as used in the British Museum records) is a term in popular use for the period of warm and cold phases between 126,000 and 11,000 years ago or, more narrowly, to the time after 40,000 years ago when fully modern humans arrived in Europe and North America.  The Pleistocene is a geological term used to refer to the same period of climatic changes as the Ice Age.  The Last Ice Age refers to the period of warm and cold phases between 126,000 and c.11, 000 years ago when fully modern people like us (Homo sapiens sapiens) spread around the earth." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Last Ice Age" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "11,000 years ago" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-9050"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "126,000 years ago" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-124050"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x117615> .

