<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hp9sx> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Urartu kingdom, eastern Turkey" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q43> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x14660> ;
    skos:altLabel "Urartian"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "An Iron Age culture of eastern Turkey named after the kingdom of Urartu (c 860-585 BC) centred around Lake Van. At its height, the Urartian kingdom extended north into modern Armenia and southern Georgia; west to the sources of the River Euphrates in Turkey; east to modern Lake Urmia and south to the sources of the River Tigris. Urartu passed into the hands of the Orontid kingdom of Armenia after invasions by Scythians and Medes from the areas of modern Ukraine, Russia, Kazakhstan and Iran around 612 BC." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Urartian" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "585 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0584"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c 860 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0859"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x14660> .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hpcqv> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Kent county and parts of East Sussex and Greater London" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x114769> ;
    skos:altLabel "British Iron Age Kent"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "This term is restricted to Iron Age numismatics, and applies to coins produced between 150 BC-AD 50. It refers to an area roughly equivalent to the modern county of Kent and parts of East Sussex and Greater London, in which similar styled coins are found. The various issuing authorities of this area seem to have relied on the same sources of technology and craftsmanship. Probable rulers in this area include Dubnovellaunos, Sam, Vosenos, Eppillus, Solidu and Amminius. The ethnic or tribal identities of these rulers and the anonymous issuers of earlier coins remain uncertain, although many modern books label them as \"Cantii\" or \"Cantiaci\". In fact, this area may well have been fragmented before the Roman period. The term \"South Eastern\" is used in Hobbs' catalogue of the BM collection (1996) but is often considered confusing.  R Hobbs, British Iron Age Coins in the British Museum, London (BMP) 1996." ;
    skos:prefLabel "British Iron Age Kent" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "AD 50" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0050"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "150 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0149"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x114769> .

