<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hpp8c> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "west central Turkey" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q43> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x14410> ;
    skos:altLabel "Phrygian"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "An Iron Age culture of west central Turkey named after the kingdom of Phrygia (c900-700 BC) with a capital at Gordion. Invasions by Cimmerians, nomads from the Ukraine and Russia, around 700BC culminated in the capture of Gordion in 696 BC, but Phrygian art and culture continued to flourish. Lydians then took over the territory in the 620s BC until the capture of Sardis by king Cyrus II in 546 BC when Lydia became part of the Achaemenid empire." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phrygian" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "546 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0545"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c 900 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0899"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x14410> .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hppss> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Afghanistan and north-western Pakistan" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q843>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q889> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x13986> ;
    skos:altLabel "Indo-Greek"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "The term refers to a specific independent Greek kingdom (c.180 BC-AD 10), ruling in Afghanistan south of the Hindu Kush mountains and north-western Pakistan up to the Indus and Sutlej rivers (i.e. including the Punjab).  The Indo-Greeks lost Afghanistan and most of Pakistan to Indo-Scythians c. 70 BC, but survived in the Punjab until c. AD 10. They are the successors to the Greco-Bactrians, who broke away from the Seleucid empire c. 250 BC to form an independent Greek kingdom in Bactria (Afghanistan north of the Hindu Kush) and Sogdiana (modern Tajikistan), which was lost to nomadic tribes of the Indo-Scythians and Yuezhi by c. 100 BC." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Indo-Greek" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "AD 10" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0010"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c. 180 BC" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0179"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x13986> .

