<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hvzgb> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "East Africa" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q27407> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x111205> ;
    skos:altLabel "Eburran"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "Period of the Later Stone Age of East Africa, particularly the Lake Nakuru - Naivasha Basin region, characterised by large backed blades, crescents, edge retouched flakes and burins mostly made from obsidian. The Eburran dates from c.13,000-8000 and is divided into 5 phases the earliest of which are pre-Neolithic and the last, phase 5, Neolithic including pottery and bones of domesticated animals. Formerly referred to as Aurignacian by Leakey at Gamble's Cave this term has also replaced Kenya Capsian." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eburran" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "8000 BP" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-6050"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c. 13,000 BP" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-11050"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x111205> .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hvzvk> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Syria, Egypt" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q79>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q858> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x52008> ;
    skos:altLabel "Ayyubid dynasty"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "1169-1260 (AH 564-AH 658). Dynasty of Kurdish origin whose most famous ruler was Saladin I, the founder of the Ayyubid dynasty in Damascus. He conquered Fatimid Egypt and replaced Isma'ili Shi'ism with strong orthodox Sunni religious and educational policy. The Ayyubids were continuing the policy of the Zangids in Syria and the earlier Great Seljuqs. After the death of the Ayyubid al-Kamil Nasir al-Din Muhammad in 1238 (AH 635) internal quarrels turned the Ayyubid empire into a confederation of principalities. The Mongol invasion put an end to Ayyubid rule." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ayyubid dynasty" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1260 (AH 658)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1260"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1169 (AH 564)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1169"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x52008> .

