<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57hwwtz> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Iraq" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q796> ;
    owl:sameAs <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x22966> ;
    skos:altLabel "Abbasid dynasty"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p08m57h> ;
    skos:note "749-1258 (AH 132-AH 923). Dynasty ruling the Muslim world as caliphs with Iraq at it's centre and Baghdad it's capital. The Abbasids derived from the family of the Prophet Muhammad's uncle al-'Abbas and the Meccan clan of Hashim. The Abbasids were influenced by older Persian traditions of divine rulership. The Abbasid revolution, while in origin an Arab movement, began on Persian soil and took advantage of discontent in the Persian lands. The shifting of the capital from Damascus under the Umayyads to Baghdad in Iraq symbolised a new eastward orientation of the caliphate with increasingly evident Persian material and cultural practives of the next centuries. The Abbasid caliphs adopted honorific titles expressing divine support for their rule. The first three centuries of Abbasid rule saw the full flowering of medieval Islamic civilisation and influences coming in from Persia and the Hellenistic and Byzantine cultures. The Abbasid ruler best known in the West is Harun al-Rashid (786-809 AH 170-193), who is associated with the stories of 1001 Nights." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Abbasid dynasty" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1258 (AH 923)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1258"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "749 (AH 132)" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0749"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    foaf:page <http://collection.britishmuseum.org/id/thesauri/x22966> .

