<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0jk4xk2gz6> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "World, especially Northern Hemisphere" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    skos:altLabel "A General Crisis c.1580 to c.1710"@en,
        "LIA"@en,
        "Little Ice Age"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Locator: p. 350" ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0jk4xk> ;
    skos:note "Source's \"Definition 3\" for \"Little Ice Age\": \"This definition narrows the LIA to its most vital effects during its strongest phase. Although cooling may have begun centuries earlier, it evidently accelerated during the late 1500s, especially in the more populous Northern Hemisphere. The cold of the early fourteenth century may have brought disasters to late medieval Northern Europe and Ming China but only as isolated incidences. From the mid-fourteenth to mid-sixteenth centuries, the demography of Eurasia was mainly determined by loss and recovery from the Black Death, and that of Amerindians by diseases of the Columbian Exchange. Because populations were far from their ecological limits, climate fluctuations had more limited impacts. The late sixteenth century brought a perfect storm of population pressures and rapid cooling. Recently recovered populations across Eurasia faced harvest failures, rising prices, and the threat or reality of famine, while early settlers in North America froze and starved in harsh winters. These pressures led to economic and political crises and then to chronic war and rebellion, first in such environmentally vulnerable regions as the Mediterranean but later across Eurasia. The mid-seventeenth century witnessed the culmination of these disasters, most of all in Europe and China but even in West Africa and Southeast Asia, justifying the designation “general crisis.” Extreme cold and famine continued through the 1690s and in the great winter of 1709. Most of the eighteenth century, however, brought a milder climate\" (p. 350)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Little Ice Age" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c. 1710" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1710"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c. 1580" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1580"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0jk4xk3k3r> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "World" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    skos:altLabel "LIA"@en,
        "Little Ice Age"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Locators: p. 327, p. 345." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0jk4xk> ;
    skos:note "Source offers at least two different extents for the broadest sense of the term \"Little Ice Age\": \"Between c.1300 and c.1850 A.D. the world became, on average, slightly but significantly colder\" (p. 327); \"Within this era of cooling, broadly defined (1251 to 1820), the world also experienced nine periods of about thirty years each with clustered volcanic and solar forcing and cooler average global temperatures\" (p. 345)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Little Ice Age" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1820 or c. 1850 A.D." ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "1820"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "1850"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1251 or c. 1300 A.D." ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "1251"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "1300"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0jk4xk9v63> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "World" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    skos:altLabel "A Cooling World c.1400 to c.1850"@en,
        "LIA"@en,
        "Little Ice Age"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Locator: p. 348." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0jk4xk> ;
    skos:note "Source's \"Definition 1\" for \"Little Ice Age\": \"Parts of Europe and Asia may have begun cooling earlier, but to avoid anthropocentric bias, the lia is identified in this context with global cooling in both hemispheres. According to the most recent data, this cooling began c.1400 and ended when the man-made greenhouse-gas effect cancelled the long-term effects of orbital forcing c.1850. This era also underwent several periods of solar/volcanic forcing, but these short-term events did not fundamentally alter the underlying cooling trend; nor were they unique to these centuries... This definition may best serve the natural sciences\" (p. 348)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Little Ice Age" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "c. 1850" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "1850"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
