<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tk5mn7> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Dura-Europos, Syria" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ bibo:locator "pg. 19-22" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q464266> ;
    skos:altLabel "Hellenistic Period"@en,
        "Seleucid Period"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "113 BCE is the date most frequently used for the start of Dura's Arsacid (Parthian) period, based on Bellinger's problematic reading of the coins. To account for Baird's preferred range of \"late second to early first century BCE\", we have expressed the relative concepts of \"early\" and \"late\" according to PeriodO's preferred practice of dividing centuries into approximately equal thirds." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tk> ;
    skos:note "Baird specifies, \"The precise date of Dura’s foundation is similarly hazy (and in any case, it did not spring fully formed into existence but came into being over a period of time); traditionally it is placed around 300 BCE, based on what we know of the settlement of Syria by the Seleucids.\" (19)...\"The subjugation of Dura under Arsacid rule has traditionally been dated to 113 BCE, based on the reading of the coin evidence by Bellinger. There is actually no secure evidence for the date at which the Arsacids took control of the site; instead, we can only presume this hegemony belongs to the late second or early first century BCE based on what is known of the general breakup of Seleucid control of the region\" (p. 23)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hellenistic Period" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "Late second or early first century BCE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "-0131"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "-0065"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "300 BCE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-0299"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tk6npz> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Dura-Europos, Syria" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ bibo:locator "pg. 23-29" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q464266> ;
    skos:altLabel "Arsacid Period"@en,
        "Parthian Period"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "113 BCE is the start date date most frequently used for Dura's Arsacid (Parthian) period, based on Bellinger's problematic reading of the coins. To account for Baird's preferred start range of \"late second to early first century BCE\", we have expressed the relative concepts of \"early\" and \"late\" according to PeriodO's preferred practice of dividing centuries into approximately equal thirds. End dates derived from discussion on pages 23-29, citing the date of Lucius Verus's 165 CE Persian campaign as marking the earliest possible moment for sustained Roman control of the city and the 190s CE marking the latest possible moment based on the fact that \"In 194, Dura fell within Emperor Septimius Severus’ new province of Syria Coele\" (p. 30)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tk> ;
    skos:note "Baird specifies, \"The subjugation of Dura under Arsacid rule has traditionally been dated to 113 BCE, based on the reading of the coin evidence by Bellinger. There is actually no secure evidence for the date at which the Arsacids took control of the site; instead, we can only presume this hegemony belongs to the late second or early first century BCE based on what is known of the general breakup of Seleucid control of the region.\" (p. 23) Arsacid control of the site was briefly interrupted by the Romans c. 116-117 CE (p. 23)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arsacid Period" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "165-190s CE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "0165"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "0194"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "late second or early first century BCE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "-0131"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "-0065"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tk739s> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Dura-Europos, Syria" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ bibo:locator "pg. 23-26" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q464266> ;
    skos:altLabel "Brief period of Roman control"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "It is unclear exactly how long the Romans held the city during this brief interlude; it may be less than a year. " ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tk> ;
    skos:note "The brief period of Roman control of the site under Trajan is attested by an inscription from a triumphal arch dedicated to Trajan whose remains were excavated outside the walls of Dura--see Preliminary Report 7/8: 128–129, no. 868; Baird also notes: \"While historical sources outside Dura are silent on Rome’s intervention at the site under Trajan, a single Greek inscription tells us that it was not a quiet nor unnoticed handover: in 116/17 CE, the inscription records that a man called Alexander (he was also known as Ammaios, it reports), who was the son of one Epinicus, renovated the naos of a temple, as his father had, and that he replaced at his own expense the temple doors, the originals having been taken away by the Romans. So, there was damage to the site (and the seizure of booty) by Roman forces, and the Romans were recorded by name as having desecrated a temple, all within Dura’s Arsacid period; this was an explicit judgement against the Romans\" (p. 23)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brief period of Roman control" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "117 CE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0117"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "116 CE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0116"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tkfskp> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Dura-Europos, Syria" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ bibo:locator "pg. 29-37" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q464266> ;
    skos:altLabel "Roman Period"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Dates derived from discussion on pages 23-29, citing the date of Lucius Verus's 165 CE Persian campaign as marking the earliest possible moment for sustained Roman control of the city and the 190s CE marking the latest possible moment based on the fact that \"In 194, Dura fell within Emperor Septimius Severus’ new province of Syria Coele\" (p. 30)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0ns3tk> ;
    skos:note "The Roman occupation of Dura \"is usually presumed to date to 165 CE, with the campaign of Lucius Verus against Parthia, and a Greek dedication to Lucius Verus was indeed found in the temple of Artemis at Dura. The situation after 165 is blurry, and it is not certain that after this date the site was under direct Roman control. Ted Kaizer has recently suggested that the territory may have been controlled not by Rome or Parthia but by Palmyra. Nevertheless, the early years of Dura under Roman rule, perhaps starting as early as 165 or as late as the 190s, are not well attested. Specifically, they are not easily archaeologically isolated due to the nature of the excavations, although there are a number of dated inscriptions and parchments within the site which anchor particular structures and events. In either case, Dura would remain under Roman control for less than a century, but it is this, final, period of the site which is the most extensively excavated and studied archaeologically.\" (p. 29) \"The traditional date of 256 CE for the fall of Dura was based on a single coin found on the person of a soldier who was buried hastily in the siege rampart.130 While this is circumstantial, there is no evidence for Roman occupation after this date\" (p. 36)." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Roman Period" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "256 CE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0256"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "165-190s CE" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ periodo:earliestYear "0165"^^xsd:gYear ;
                    periodo:latestYear "0194"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

