<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwkkmk9> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Levant" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q81483> ;
    skos:altLabel "Nebekian"@en ;
    skos:broader <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwks8k2> ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwk> ;
    skos:note """Typical tools include finely retouched, elongated, narrow bladelets. Pieces are either arch-backed or obliquely truncated. Bladelets are fragmented due to the microburin technique and represent the oldest examples showing the habitual use of this technique in the Levant. Summary: Elongated backed bladelets, arch-backed bladelets and microliths, First common use of microburin technique (both classical and Krukowski type).

The term Nebekian was first used by Rust (1950) for the artifacts he excavated in Yabroud, Shelter 3, layers 6 and 7. Madamaghan sites in southern Jordan may belong to the Nebekian if the lunates of these inventories are indeed intrusive. The distribution of the Nebekian seems to be towards the eastern steppic region of the Levant, even though the low number of sites makes this assumption diffcult to prove.""" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nebekian" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "21000 BP2000" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-19000"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "24000 BP2000" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-22000"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

