<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwkrsmz> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Central Africa" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1000>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1009>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q657>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q929>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q971>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q974>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q983> ;
    skos:altLabel "KBS Industry"@en,
        "Kay Behrensmeyer Site Industry"@en ;
    skos:broader <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwknm9c> ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwk> ;
    skos:note """Like Oldowan but with rarity of retouched flakes (small scrapers).

Industry deriving from the Kay Behrensmeyer Site (KBS) tuff and KBS member of Koobi Fora. The assemblages are comparable to Olduvai Bed I. The only difference – and therefore the distinction – is the rarity of retouched flakes (small scrapers). But since these assemblages are broadly contemporary, the KBS Industry should be seen as a local variant of the Oldowan industry.""" ;
    skos:prefLabel "KBS Industry" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1800000 BP2000" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-1798000"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "1900000 BP2000" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-1898000"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwksjfw> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Levant" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q81483> ;
    skos:altLabel "Natufian"@en ;
    skos:broader <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwks8k2> ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0s2rwk> ;
    skos:note """Natufian most widely known Epipalaeolithic industry in Near East, already discovered in the 1920s. Ground stone tools and blades with sickle gloss indicate use and cultivation of cereals. Summary: Use of classical and Krukowski microburin technique, Lunates, size and style Helwan retouch or simple backing, Picks and sickle blades with gloss, Regular use of ground stone tools like mortars, mullers and pestles.

Natufian occurs in central and northern Palestine, with fewer finds in southern Palestine, the steppe of the Negev and Sinai, high Lebanon and Anti-Lebanon mountains, and Syro-Arabian Desert. Microliths and geometrics make up >40% of every assemblage.""" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Natufian" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "11000 BP2000" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-9000"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "15000 BP2000" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "-13000"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

