<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p04k9nh58qr> a skos:Concept ;
    periodo:spatialCoverageDescription "Western Panama" ;
    dcelements:language "en" ;
    dcterms:language <http://lexvo.org/id/iso639-1/en> ;
    dcterms:source [ bibo:locator "pp. 74-75" ] ;
    dcterms:spatial <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q804> ;
    skos:altLabel "Aguas Buenas"@en,
        "Barriles Phase"@en,
        "Bugaba Phase"@en,
        "Burica Phase"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "While Palumbo presents “Aguas Buenas” as the primary label in the text, he focuses elsewhere on the term “Bugaba”. He also underlines the heterogeneity of interpretation concerning this period in scholarship on the area; differences emerge in the consideration of the period as a stylistic, demographic/ethic, or political phenomenon." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p04k9nh> ;
    skos:note """“For decades, the A.D. 600  eruption of Volcán Barú was assumed to have been catastrophic enough to have ended Aguas Buenas settlements across much of the region (Linares 1977:313, 1980e:245; Sheets 2004)”, but “it appears that both the A.D. 600 eruption and assumptions about the lack of early populations in the Caribbean watershed need to be rethought. Like the rest of Gran Chiriquí, the Aguas Buenas in Gran Chiriquí might have lasted until A.D. 800 or later (Corrales 2000).” (pp 74-75) 

“In Western Panama, the Aguas Buenas has three different names. The most used is the Bugaba (with early and late halves), coined by Spang et al. (1980) to refer to ceramics in highland Chiriquí and the Caribbean coast (Kudarauskas et al. 1980). The second is the Burica (A.D. 400-600), used by Linares (1968) to describe Aguas Buenas materials on the Pacific coast. The least used is the Barriles phase (400  B.C. to A.D. 200), described by Haberland  (1976).” (p. 75)""" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aguas Buenas" ;
    time:intervalFinishedBy [ skos:prefLabel "A.D. 900" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0900"^^xsd:gYear ] ] ;
    time:intervalStartedBy [ skos:prefLabel "A.D. 300" ;
            time:hasDateTimeDescription [ time:year "0300"^^xsd:gYear ] ] .

