    dcterms:title "The periodization of history." .

<http://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p0367jz> a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    dcterms:source <http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/79745128> ;
    skos:editorialNote """Full reference: Ephraim Emerton, "The Periodization of History", Proceedings of the Massachusetts Historical Society (October 1918-July 1919), 52: 55-69. Available online at https://www.google.com/books/edition/Proceedings_of_the_Massachusetts_Histori/cfwKAAAAIAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0. Spatial coverage in this work balances repeated references to "the world" by the author (taken as the source's assertion of coverage) with the apparently exclusive focus of the periods mentioned on Europe (as at p. 59, "If we confine our interest to that branch of the stream of history which chiefly interests us as heirs of the European tradition, we find ready to our hand the conventional division in to 'ancient', 'mediaeval', and 'modern'").

Emerton's paper actually proposes only five broad divisions of history, but he refers to the labels for the 16th and 18th centuries as common knowledge, so this work also attests to the currency of those period terms at the time of publication.""" .

